then you can run planning with respect  to that goal and it's going to give you  it's going to say that this prediction  looks pretty good in terms of  accomplishing the subjective  and then you'll run  um you run these actions on the robot  and uh you'll get  behavior that looks something like this  now one of the things that's cool about  this is that you could actually use a  single model not just to solve this one  task but lots of different tasks  so  um you can give it a goal of picking up  the the stuffed animal on the left  you can give it a goal of moving the  sleeve of the shirt over to the right  putting an apple on a plate  and essentially it's going to use its  model to plan to accomplish these  different goals at test time I mean you  don't even need to tell it these tasks  during training  yeah  yeah  so the question is is there a reason why  the robot is moving in discrete steps  um the I guess there's a few different  ways to actually do robot control one is  in a more like a very synchronous  fashion where you move and then you pick  an action and then you move again and  you pick an action you could also do it  asynchronously  um  one of the things that's uh I guess if  you're replanning at every time step  um that planning time might take some  computation time  um and the  uh  I guess there's a few few things here  but one is that if you want to replant  every time step for that current image  then it's easiest to do things  synchronously  um it's you can in principle do it  asynchronously but it gets uh more  complicated  and you also need a control for the  frequency at which you collected the  data and the frequency that you're  actually rolling out the actions at test  time  because you don't want to have a  mismatch in your control frequency  because then you'll have like a mismatch  in the data distribution  um and here are kind of a few more  examples of tasks  um including the last example of like  uh folding a towel over an object  cool  um so in terms of modeling directly in  observation space  um one thing that's pretty nice about  this is  um I guess in comparison to some of the  other approaches this is operating on  real images  it also requires pretty little human  involvement  um  the train is video production model is  like fully self-supervised on your data  and you can also accomplish a lot of  different tasks with a single model  the downside is that  um if you have a lot of background  variability it's going to be much harder  to learn a good model  and this kind of planning approach here  can't handle as complex skills as as  other methods because it's just using  like a random shooting planner and uh  yeah more sophisticated planners and so  forth can accomplish more  um  more challenging skills also uh because  the model is so large you can't roll out  that many samples uh unless you have a  ton of compute basically so it's fairly  compute intensive it has time  okay um and the last thing I'd like to  mention is that I instead of predicting  future States or future observations  um in some cases you can also predict  other quantities  and there's a few different kind of  instantiations of this so if you want to  learn how to grasp you could try to  predict like  if I take these actions will my grasp be  successful  or if I take a sequence of actions will  I Collide into something or if I take a  sequence of actions like what were my  help what my health be what will my  damage be and so forth  um you can essentially think of this as  a different variant of model based Earl  whereas instead of predicting  the state into the future you're  predicting some statistics about that  state  um and then you can use the same kinds  of planning approaches with these  predictive models  um this also has a fairly close  connection to key learning so if you  think about the reward being the  probability of some event happening then  these kinds of approaches are producing  the probabilities of events and so  in that case they're uh  essentially predicting future reward and  few functions are predicting future  reward as well  um so the upside of this approach is  that you're potentially only predicting  these task relevant quantities and this  can be quite helpful quite useful when  you have very like complex observation  spaces  uh the downside is that you need to have  you need to be able to observe those  quantities you need to have labels of  those quantities in your data  um and you of course you need to pick  the qualities somewhat manually  great um so to wrap up we talked about  model based RL in this lecture and we  also talked about model free learning  previously and so I'd like to illustrate  some of the pros and cons of these  approaches  for models it's pretty easy to collect  data in a self-supervised way and train  the model in the self-supervised way  um these models are very transferable  across different reward functions  and typically they require a smaller  quantity of reward supervised data  because you're offloading a lot of the  effort onto learning the model rather  than learning a model of reward  um the downside is that these models are  optimizing for being able to predict the  future and not for task performance  and sometimes it's harder to learn a  model than it is to learn a policy  um and then lastly it you sometimes need  assumptions in order to learn more  complex skills because uh the models for  those more complex skills might also be  fairly complex  um in the model free case uh it makes  very little assumptions  um  with regard to like the complexity of  your observation space the complexity of  your Dynamics and so forth and it's  fairly effective for learning complex  policies  the downside is that it ends up  requiring more experience typically and  it's also a a harder optimization  problem in the multitask setting because  you have to learn kind of multiple Cube  like you're popular like a multitask  queue function or a multitask policy  um ultimately I think that we in many  scenarios probably want elements of both  if we can learn a good model then we  should use that  um  but it would also be nice to have some  of the kind of pros of model free  methods  great  um so that concludes the main content  for today  um for next time uh we've talked about  multitask girl so far and so next week  on Monday and Wednesday we'll talk about  meta reinforcement learning and then as  a couple of reminders the mid quarter  survey is out and uh homework free is  out as well
 

